%S A110375 11269,11566,12376,12430,12700,12754,15013,17589,17797,18181,18421,
%T A110375 18453,18549,18597,18885,18949,18997,20865,21531,21721,21963,22683,
%U A110375 23421,23457,23547,23691,23729,23853,24015,24087,24231,24339,24519,24591,24627,24681,24825,24933,25005,25023,25059,25185,25293,27020
%N A110375 Numbers n such that Maple 9.5, Maple 10, Maple 11 and Maple 12 give the wrong answers for the number of partitions of n.
%C A110375 Based on various postings on the Web, sent to _N. J. A. Sloane_ by _R. J. Mathar_. Thanks to several correspondents who sent information about other versions of Maple.
%C A110375 Mathematica 6.0, DrScheme and PARI 2.3.3 all give the correct answers.
%C A110375 Ramanujan's congruence says that numbpart(5*k+4) == 0 (mod 5), so numbpart(11269) = ...851 == 1 (mod 5) can't be correct. - _Robert Gerbicz_, May 13 2008
%C A110375 From _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 18 2026: (Start)
%C A110375 Some noteworthy information (mainly from the MaplePrimes link):
%C A110375 The error stems from a premature truncation of Rademacher's infinite sum. Reducing the tolerance with `numbpart := subs(0.1=0.03, eval(combinat[numbpart]))` gave correct results in the given range (but maybe again errors for larger indices). Using 0.05 instead of 0.1 gave a correct result for all the listed indices except one, interestingly enough among the smaller values.
%C A110375 Maple's wrong values were off by 1 from the correct values. The correct last 2 digits of the values for the listed indices are [50, 48, 86, 95, 91, 45, 81, 90, 83, 54, 77, 59, 00, 15, 35, 00, 12, 88, 94, 79, 14, 63, 68, 44, 28, 33, 05, 61, 74, 69, 06, 45, 15, 55, 18, 07, 06, 34, 00, 01, 40, 80, 01, 13].
%C A110375 Ramanujan's congruences state that for indices of the form 5*k+4, 7*k+5 and 11*k+6, the result must be a multiple of the prime factor (5, 7 resp. 11). This allows checking and correcting about half of the wrong results.
%C A110375 The correct numbpart(11269) = 2 * 25 * 7 * 691 * P108 with a 108-digit prime factor, while (the wrong) numbpart(11269)+1 = 13 * 151 * 193 * 5710919 * 6690143 * C95, with a 95-digit composite factor with no known prime factor (as of today). (End)
%H A110375 Alec Mihailovs and others, <a href="https://www.mapleprimes.com/posts/39381-A110375">Concerning this sequence</a>, MaplePrimes forum, May-Dec. 2008
%H A110375 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramanujan%27s_congruences">Ramanujan's congruences</a>. Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, Dec. 2025, updated Sep. 2026.
%e A110375 From PARI, the correct answer:
%e A110375 numbpart(11269)
%e A110375 2311391772313039755144117876494556289590601993601099725578515191051551761\
%e A110375 80318215891795874905318274163248033071850
%e A110375 From Maple 11, incorrect:
%e A110375 combinat[numbpart](11269);
%e A110375 2311391772313039755144117876494556289590601993601099725578515191051551761\
%e A110375 80318215891795874905318274163248033071851
%e A110375 On the other hand, the old Maple 6 gives the correct answer.
